#WRITES
#AUTO 50
#NEEDED 1
# Display controller (VOP2) presence + alive check.
#
# A layer-4 probe: the VOP2 register block is dark until its clocks
# are ungated, and reading it while gated hangs the bus — so the
# ungate writes MUST come first. We ungate the VOP2 + VO bus clocks,
# make sure the VOP resets are released, then read the VOP2 version
# register, which holds a fixed version constant. If it reads that
# constant, the VOP2 is powered, clocked, and out of reset;
# 0x00000000 or garbage means it isn't.
#
# Requires --writes (the ungate / reset-release are CRU writes).
#
# Register facts (RK3568 TRM, verified):
#   CLKGATE_CON20 @ 0xFDD20350 — VOP clocks, 0=on/1=gated. The
#     load-bearing bits are 2..12 (VOP + VO bus-interface units);
#     gating any of the BIU clocks hangs AXI/AHB access.
#   SOFTRST_CON16 @ 0xFDD20440 — VOP resets, 1=held/0=released,
#     bits 0..8.
#   VOP2_SYS_VERSION_INFO @ 0xFE040004 — reads 0x40158023 on this
#     board (observed in the first probe sweep, 2026-06-29). The
#     RK3568 TRM lists 0x40158588 for a different VOP2 revision; the
#     high half (0x4015_8xxx) matches, the low half is the sub-rev.

# Snapshot the two CRU registers as-found BEFORE we touch them (issue 110o),
# so the restore at the bottom returns them to exactly this state — even if
# the display was already turned on for other reasons and our ungate/release
# below changes nothing. The point is to leave it as we found it, on or off.
SAVE 0xFDD20350
SAVE 0xFDD20440
# And note whether they were still at their power-on defaults when we arrived.
# CLKGATE_CON20 bits 2..12 default to 1 (gated); SOFTRST_CON16 bits 0..8
# default to 1 (held), per the RK3568 TRM. A NON-DEFAULT result just means
# something (the bootloader, another subsystem) already brought the display
# up — the SAVE above still captured the true as-found state to restore to.
DEFAULT 0xFDD20350 0x00001FFC 0x00001FFC
DEFAULT 0xFDD20440 0x000001FF 0x000001FF

LOG ungate VOP2 + VO bus clocks (CLKGATE_CON20 bits 2..12 -> 0)
W 0xFDD20350 0x1FFC0000
LOG release VOP2 resets (SOFTRST_CON16 bits 0..8 -> 0)
W 0xFDD20440 0x01FF0000
DELAY 20000

LOG read VOP2 version — expect 0x40158023 if alive and clocked
EXPECT 0xFE040004 0x40158023 0xFFFFFFFF
R 0xFE040004

# Put the display block back exactly as we found it (issue 110o): restore the
# saved reset register, then the saved clock gate. The maskbits (0x01FF, 0x1FFC)
# name the bits we touched; RESTORE writes them in write-mask form, which is
# mandatory for these CRU registers — a raw write-back of a masked register
# no-ops. If the display was already on when we arrived, these restore the
# on-state (a no-op); if we turned it on here, they turn it back off.
LOG restore VOP2 reset register to as-found
RESTORE 0xFDD20440 0x01FF
LOG restore VOP2 clock gate to as-found
RESTORE 0xFDD20350 0x1FFC

LOG display presence probe complete
